{
  "dom_id": "question-168888",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248290/assets/1d2132be4de3f505e5052ae3083b75ac8aceceed9f64bf62d128746a37a59a23.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local minimum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local minimum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nAmong the given options, such neighborhoods exist for the point $C.$ Hence, $C$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248290/assets/a389560d6217e86b82384443c5730168483a75a1759788ddde31ef64ad368adc.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
